--- baseline/leadership_summary_text
+++ new/leadership_summary_text
@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+I lead technology the way I'd want it led if I were the CFO or CEO reading the board deck: plain language, honest numbers, and a roadmap that ties back to what the business is actually trying to do. At SK Life Science I built the IT organization essentially from nothing in 2018 and carried it through our first U.S. product launch to support $1B in annual commercial operations, so I understand what MBX is heading into and the sequencing that gets a company there without breaking compliance or the budget along the way. I report to the CFO by instinct as much as by org chart - I'd rather bring a fully costed option with a recommendation than a menu of choices. On AI specifically, I've kept it useful rather than fashionable: our security automation and next-best-action work in Medical Affairs delivered real savings, but validated systems stayed the system of record and a person signed off before anything acted on the output. I hire senior people and then leave them room to run their areas, which is how I grew a 10-person function to 38 without becoming a bottleneck myself - my job was governance, prioritization, and standing in front of the executive committee defending what we were spending and why. Cybersecurity, data governance, and audit readiness aren't a separate program in my view; they're built into how the platforms are chosen and run from day one.